0	189	Dalia Grybauskaitė (Lithuanian pronunciation: [dɐˈlʲɛ ɡʲrʲiːbɐʊsˈkɐ̂ˑɪtʲeː]; born 1 March 1956) is a Lithuanian politician who served as the eighth president of Lithuania from 2009 to 2019.
190	353	She is the first and so far only woman to hold the position and in 2014 she became the first President of Lithuania to be reelected for a second consecutive term.

353	493	Grybauskaitė has served as Minister of Finance, as well as European Commissioner for Financial Programming and the Budget from 2004 to 2009.
494	565	She is often referred to as the "Iron Lady" or the "Steel Magnolia".



565	694	Early years
Grybauskaitė was born on 1 March 1956 to a working-class family in Vilnius during the Soviet occupation of Lithuania.
695	797	Her mother, Vitalija Korsakaitė (1922–1989), was born in the Biržai region and worked as a saleswoman.
798	875	Her father, Polikarpas Grybauskas (1928–2008), was an electrician and driver.
876	934	He also was a NKVD serviceman during the Second World War.
935	984	Grybauskaitė attended Salomėja Nėris High School.
985	1095	She has two brothers, one living in Lithuania, and the other living in Colorado Springs, in the United States.
1096	1222	She has described herself as not among the best students, receiving mostly fours in a system where five was the highest grade.
1223	1283	Her favourite subjects were history, geography and physics.

1283	1390	Grybauskaitė began participating in sports at the age of eleven, and became a passionate basketball player.
1391	1506	At the age of nineteen, she worked for a year at the Lithuanian National Philharmonic Society as a staff inspector.
1507	1607	She then enrolled in A. A. Zhdanov State University in Leningrad, as a student of political economy.
1608	1676	At the same time, she began working in a local factory in Leningrad.
1677	1807	In 1983, Grybauskaitė graduated with a citation and returned to Vilnius, taking a secretarial position at the Academy of Sciences.
1808	1961	Work in the academy was scarce and so she moved to the Vilnius Communist Party High School, where she lectured in political economics and global finance.
1962	2178	From 1983 to December 1989, she was a member of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union and after the Communist Party of Lithuania broke away from the CPSU in December 1989, she was member of the CPL 
2179	2256	In 1988, she defended her PhD thesis at Moscow (Academy of Social Sciences).

2256	2517	In 1990, soon after Lithuania reestablished its independence from the Soviet Union, Grybauskaitė continued her studies at the Edmund A. Walsh School of Foreign Service at Georgetown University, Washin
2517	2693	Early career
Between 1991 and 1993, Grybauskaitė worked as Director of the European Department at the Ministry of International Economic Relations of the Republic of Lithuania.
2694	2881	During 1993, she was employed in the Foreign Ministry as director of the Economic Relations Department, and represented Lithuania when it entered the European Union free trade agreements.
2882	2950	She also chaired the Aid Coordination Committee (Phare and the G24).
2951	3067	Soon afterwards, she was named Extraordinary Envoy and Plenipotentiary Minister at the Lithuanian Mission to the EU.
3068	3216	There, she worked as the deputy chief negotiator for the EU Europe Agreement and as a representative of the National Aid Co-ordination in Brussels.

3216	3318	In 1996, Grybauskaitė was appointed Plenipotentiary Minister in the United States' Lithuanian embassy.
3319	3404	She held this position until 1999, when she was appointed deputy Minister of Finance.
3405	3511	As part of this role, she led Lithuanian negotiations with the World Bank and International Monetary Fund.
3512	3660	In 2000, Grybauskaitė became Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs, going on in 2001 to become Minister of Finance in the Algirdas Brazauskas government.
3661	3782	Lithuania joined the European Union on 1 May 2004, and Grybauskaitė was named a European Commissioner on the same day.



3782	3907	European Commission
Grybauskaitė initially served as European Commissioner for Education, Culture, Multilingualism and Youth.
3908	4085	She held this position until 11 November 2004, when she was named European Commissioner for Financial Programming and the Budget within the José Manuel Barroso-led Commission.


4085	4202	In November 2005, Grybauskaitė was named "Commissioner of the Year" in the European Voice Europeans of the Year poll.
4203	4354	She was nominated "for her unrelenting efforts to shift EU spending towards areas that would enhance competitiveness such as research and development."
4355	4453	She commented:I don't usually participate in contests, so this is a very pleasant surprise for me.
4454	4642	I consider it a distinction not for me personally, but for all the new EU Member States, both small and large, as an acknowledgment of their bringing a new and fresh perspective to the EU.
4643	4796	I think that it's also a prize for having the courage to speak the often difficult truth and to point out the real price of political rhetoric in Europe.
4797	4844	As for results, we still have to wait for them.
4845	4933	An agreement on the budget for 2007–2013, which Europe really needs, is most important.

4933	5061	As Financial and Budget Commissioner, she strongly criticized the EU budget, stating it was "not a budget for the 21st century."
5062	5129	The majority of the EU budget was spent on agricultural programmes.
